@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Markers
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Services
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.State
@using DLR.Core.Display
@using DLR.Core.Tracks
@using SkiaSharp
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject RouteStyleState RouteStyles
@implements IAsyncDisposable

@*
	The one drawing surface for every authored map element (§4.5 v0.21, §16.3).

	One C# component, one SkiaSharp canvas, one set of pixels drawn on every host. The
	half of the map "we own" is drawn once, so a marker looks the same on every platform
	and the class of bug v0.13 warned about — two map code paths drift on marker
	rendering — is closed by construction.

	v0.24 consolidated the base map onto MapLibre everywhere, which makes that guarantee
	cheaper but not redundant: this component is still the only thing that draws authored
	content, and it is what a future offline or car renderer would keep (§4.5, §13 Q26).

	The overlay projects lat / lon → screen space using Web Mercator (EPSG:3857), which
	is the projection the base map renders natively. Every ViewportChanged from the base
	map is a repaint here.
*@

@*
	A plain <canvas>, not an <SKCanvasView>.

	SkiaSharp.Views.Blazor's SKCanvasView initialises through [JSImport] — WebAssembly-only —
	so on a MAUI BlazorWebView it threw on first render and took the Blazor renderer down with
	it (§4.5 v0.24). Skia itself runs perfectly well on the phone; only the canvas binding was
	browser-only. So the paint path below is unchanged and rasterises off-screen instead, and
	this element is just where the finished frame lands, via map/overlay.js.
*@
<canvas @ref="_surface" class="dlr-map-overlay" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>

@if (_error is not null)
{
	@*
		Deliberately visible. Without the overlay there are no rider pins, no markers and no
		route — on a live group ride that is the whole point of the screen, so an empty map must
		not be left to read as "nobody is moving" (§4.5, §5.3, §16.3).

		This exists because its absence cost a debugging round trip: repaints run detached from
		the render loop, so a failure inside one used to become an unobserved task exception and
		the overlay simply drew nothing, on a device, with no way to ask why.
	*@
	<div class="dlr-map-overlay-error" role="alert">
		<strong>Map markers unavailable</strong>
		<p>The base map is working, but pins, tracks and traveller positions could not be drawn.</p>
		<p class="dlr-map-overlay-error-detail">@_error</p>
	</div>
}

@code {
	/// <summary>
	/// Routes drawn under the pins, in the order they should stack — later ones over earlier.
	/// A ride may have several planned routes (§5.4); a track detail has exactly one.
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<RouteOverlay>? Routes { get; set; }

	/// <summary>Markers to draw. Ordered by ascending latitude so higher pins draw over lower.</summary>
	[Parameter] public IReadOnlyDictionary<Guid, MapMarker>? Markers { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// Ground circles drawn beneath everything else — the private-area picker's radius (§10.1)
	/// is the only caller today. Under the routes and pins because a circle is an area the other
	/// two are read against, and a wash over the top of a line would recolour it.
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<MapCircle>? Circles { get; set; }

	/// <summary>The base map's current view. Passed in by <c>RideMap.razor</c>.</summary>
	[Parameter] public MapViewport Viewport { get; set; }

	private const string ModulePath = "./_content/BlazorDLR.Shared/map/overlay.js";

	/// <summary>Where the rasterised frame lands. Owned by the browser, written through JS.</summary>
	private ElementReference _surface;

	private IJSObjectReference? _module;

	/// <summary>
	/// Why the last repaint failed, or null. Rendered rather than swallowed — see the markup.
	/// </summary>
	private string? _error;

	/// <summary>
	/// Set while a paint is encoding or marshalling.
	/// <para>
	/// A repaint costs a Skia rasterise, a PNG encode and a base64 hop across the JS bridge,
	/// and viewport events arrive once per animation frame during a drag. Without this the
	/// queue grows without bound and the overlay ends up rendering a pan that finished seconds
	/// ago. Dropping the intermediate frames is correct: only the newest one is wanted, and
	/// <see cref="_repaintPending"/> is what guarantees it is not the one dropped.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private bool _painting;

	/// <summary>A viewport or content change arrived mid-paint; repaint once the current one lands.</summary>
	private bool _repaintPending;

	/// <summary>
	/// One route's line, decoded and ready to project. Rebuilt on every parameter set.
	/// <para>
	/// The colour is kept as the caller's <em>request</em> — the palette entry and the track it
	/// belongs to — rather than as a resolved <see cref="SKColor"/>. What actually gets drawn
	/// depends on the device's preferences, which change without any parameter changing, so
	/// resolving at decode time would leave a repaint showing the previous answer.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private readonly record struct DecodedRoute(
		IReadOnlyList<(double Latitude, double Longitude)> Points,
		Guid? TrackId,
		string PaletteColour);

	/// <summary>
	/// Scratch space for one route's points in canvas coordinates. Reused across routes and
	/// frames: the line is walked twice per frame — once to build the path, once to space the
	/// direction chevrons along it — and projecting several thousand points twice per repaint,
	/// on a surface that repaints on every pan, is exactly the cost this buffer removes.
	/// </summary>
	private readonly List<SKPoint> _projected = [];

	/// <summary>
	/// The builder every per-frame path is assembled through. Paths are immutable in Skia now —
	/// they are built here and taken with <see cref="SKPathBuilder.Detach"/>, which hands over the
	/// path and leaves this empty for the next one.
	/// <para>
	/// Held rather than newed per path for the same reason the paints below are: a twenty-rider
	/// map at 1 Hz builds a pill each per frame, and a builder each would be twenty native objects
	/// a second bought for nothing. Safe as one instance because painting is synchronous and no
	/// path is built while another is part-built.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private readonly SKPathBuilder _pathBuilder = new();

	private IReadOnlyList<DecodedRoute> _decodedRoutes = [];

	/// <summary>
	/// Decoded lines, keyed on the encoded string they came from. Encoded polylines are large
	/// but stable, so a ride whose positions arrive once a second must not re-decode several
	/// thousand points per frame. Keyed rather than indexed so adding a route does not
	/// invalidate the ones already decoded.
	/// </summary>
	private readonly Dictionary<string, IReadOnlyList<(double Latitude, double Longitude)>> _lineCache =
		new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

	/// <summary>
	/// Subscribes to the device's route-style preference (§18.6). The style is not a parameter
	/// — no caller of this component knows or should know about it, and threading it down from
	/// four pages would be four places to forget it — so the change signal comes from the
	/// shared state instead, and lands as a repaint.
	/// </summary>
	protected override void OnInitialized() => RouteStyles.Changed += OnRouteStyleChanged;

	private void OnRouteStyleChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(RepaintAsync);

	protected override void OnParametersSet()
	{
		if (Routes is not { Count: > 0 } routes)
		{
			_decodedRoutes = [];
			_lineCache.Clear();
		}
		else
		{
			List<DecodedRoute> decoded = new(routes.Count);
			HashSet<string> live = new(routes.Count, StringComparer.Ordinal);

			foreach (RouteOverlay route in routes)
			{
				live.Add(route.EncodedPolyline);

				if (!_lineCache.TryGetValue(route.EncodedPolyline, out IReadOnlyList<(double, double)>? points))
				{
					// PolylineCodec is the only implementation of this encoding (§15.5) and the
					// one the server encodes with. A second decoder here is how the line ended up
					// drawn at a tenth of its coordinates — precision 5 against a precision 6
					// encoder puts a Sydney ride off the Gulf of Guinea, so the map looked empty
					// rather than wrong.
					points = PolylineCodec.DecodePoints(route.EncodedPolyline);
					_lineCache[route.EncodedPolyline] = points;
				}

				decoded.Add(new DecodedRoute(points, route.TrackId, route.Colour));
			}

			// A route detached is a line nobody will ask for again. Without this the editor —
			// which hands this component a freshly encoded line on every cursor drag — would
			// accumulate a decoded copy of every intermediate state it ever showed.
			if (_lineCache.Count > live.Count)
			{
				foreach (string stale in _lineCache.Keys.Where(key => !live.Contains(key)).ToList())
				{
					_lineCache.Remove(stale);
				}
			}

			_decodedRoutes = decoded;
		}

		// Every change repaints. There is no CSS-transform shortcut for a pan or a zoom any
		// more: the first version of this had one, and on device it put the track in the wrong
		// place and let it spill outside the map. A transform that is subtly wrong is worse
		// than a repaint that costs more, because it draws a marker somewhere the rider will
		// believe. RepaintAsync coalesces, which is what keeps the cost bounded during a drag.
		_ = InvokeAsync(RepaintAsync);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Rasterise any icon this overlay is about to draw but does not have yet, then repaint.
	/// Runs after render because it needs JS, and painting itself is synchronous — the first
	/// frame of a newly-seen icon shows the plain pin, the next shows the artwork.
	/// </summary>
	protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
	{
		if (firstRender)
		{
			// After first render, not in OnInitialized: the web binding reads localStorage
			// through JS interop, which is unreachable during the prerender pass. Idempotent,
			// so whichever map or settings panel gets here first pays for the read and the
			// rest are free. The Changed it raises is what repaints this canvas.
			await RouteStyles.LoadAsync();
		}

		if (Markers is null || Markers.Count == 0)
		{
			return;
		}

		// Runs on every render, which on a live ride is at least once a second. Collect only
		// what is genuinely new — riders draw as darts and never ask for an icon at all — so
		// the steady state is a handful of hash lookups and no allocation.
		List<string>? wanted = null;
		foreach (MapMarker marker in Markers.Values)
		{
			if (marker.Kind is MarkerKind.Rider or MarkerKind.Self)
			{
				continue;
			}

			// Keyed on the raw key, not on the resolved asset: an unrecognised key gets its own
			// cache entry holding the note icon's pixels, which costs a few KB and keeps both
			// the lookup and the "have I asked for this yet" test a single string compare.
			if (_requested.Add(marker.IconKey))
			{
				(wanted ??= []).Add(marker.IconKey);
			}
		}

		if (wanted is not null && await MarkerIconCache.PrimeAsync(wanted, JS))
		{
			// An icon arriving changes what gets drawn without changing any parameter, so this
			// goes straight to a repaint rather than through the tracking decision.
			await RepaintAsync();
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Icon keys this overlay has already handed to the cache. Keyed per component because the
	/// cache itself may answer "unavailable", and asking again every render would be the
	/// waste this set exists to avoid.
	/// </summary>
	private readonly HashSet<string> _requested = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

	/// <summary>
	/// Draws one frame. Unchanged by v0.24's move off <c>SKCanvasView</c> — it took an
	/// <c>SKCanvas</c> then and it takes one now, which is the whole reason the switch cost a
	/// surface and not a rendering rewrite.
	/// </summary>
	/// <summary>
	/// Canvas pixels per CSS pixel, for the frame being drawn.
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>Every length in this component is multiplied by this</strong>, and getting it
	/// wrong is what made the overlay unreadable on both phones. The canvas is sized in
	/// <em>device</em> pixels — that is what <see cref="MapViewport.CanvasWidthPx"/> counts, and
	/// what <c>ProjectToCanvas</c> plots into — but every width, radius and font size here was
	/// written as a CSS pixel, tuned on a desktop browser where the two are the same number.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// On a phone they are not. At the ratio of 3 a typical iPhone reports, a 2 px circle ring
	/// is two thirds of one CSS pixel and a 12 px label is 4: the tracks read as hairlines, and
	/// the private-area circle — a sheer wash behind a sub-pixel ring — did not appear at all.
	/// Positions were always right, because the projection works in the same device pixels the
	/// canvas is sized in. Only the sizes were wrong.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Ratios are deliberately <em>not</em> scaled: <c>MarkerArrowScale</c> is a proportion of
	/// an arrow, and <c>CircleWashAlpha</c> is an opacity. Scaling either would be a second bug
	/// wearing this one's clothes.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private float _scale = 1f;

	private void PaintOverlay(SKCanvas canvas)
	{
		canvas.Clear(SKColors.Transparent);

		if (Viewport.CanvasWidthPx <= 0 || Viewport.CanvasHeightPx <= 0)
		{
			return;
		}

		// Clamped rather than trusted. A viewport that arrives before the base map has measured
		// itself can carry 0, and a zero scale collapses every stroke to nothing — the same
		// invisible-overlay symptom this field exists to fix, arriving by a different route.
		_scale = Viewport.DevicePixelRatio is > 0 and <= 8
			? (float)Viewport.DevicePixelRatio
			: 1f;

		EnsurePathsMatchScale();

		LabelFont.Size = LabelFontCssPx * _scale;
		PinRing.StrokeWidth = 2f * _scale;
		LabelHalo.StrokeWidth = 3f * _scale;
		ArrowHalo.StrokeWidth = 5f * _scale;
		ArrowEdge.StrokeWidth = 2f * _scale;

		DrawCircles(canvas);
		DrawRoutes(canvas);
		DrawMarkers(canvas);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Rasterise the current viewport off-screen and hand the frame to the browser.
	/// <para>
	/// Coalescing rather than queueing: a drag produces a viewport per animation frame and a
	/// repaint costs far more than a frame, so all but the newest request is dropped and a
	/// trailing one is guaranteed by <see cref="_repaintPending"/>. During a drag that means
	/// the overlay lags the tiles slightly and then settles — chosen over the CSS-transform
	/// shortcut the first version had, which tracked smoothly and put pins in the wrong place.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task RepaintAsync()
	{
		if (_painting)
		{
			_repaintPending = true;
			return;
		}

		_painting = true;
		try
		{
			do
			{
				_repaintPending = false;

				MapViewport target = Viewport;
				string? frame = Rasterise(target);
				if (frame is null)
				{
					return;
				}

				IJSObjectReference module = await LoadModuleAsync();

				// The frame travels with the view it was drawn for. JS needs that to follow the
				// map with these pixels until the next frame arrives, which is what stops a pan
				// dragging the overlay behind it — the round trip that produced this frame is
				// far too slow to be in the loop at gesture frame rate.
				//
				// One options object rather than six positional arguments, matching createMap in
				// map.maplibre.js — the convention this folder already had.
				await module.InvokeVoidAsync("present", _surface, frame, new
				{
					widthPx = target.CanvasWidthPx,
					heightPx = target.CanvasHeightPx,
					centreLat = target.CentreLatitude,
					centreLon = target.CentreLongitude,
					zoom = target.ZoomLevel,
					bearingDeg = target.HeadingDeg,
				});

				ClearError();
			}
			while (_repaintPending);
		}
		catch (JSDisconnectedException)
		{
			// Navigating away mid-encode. Nothing to show and nothing to report.
		}
		catch (ObjectDisposedException)
		{
			// Same, from the module reference losing its race with DisposeAsync.
		}
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			// Anything else is reported, not swallowed.
			//
			// This runs detached from the render loop — OnParametersSet starts it and does not
			// await it — so without this catch a failure became an unobserved task exception
			// and the overlay just drew nothing. That is precisely the "blank rectangle rather
			// than a stated error" §4.5 forbids, and it cost a debugging round trip on a device
			// where there was no other way to ask what had gone wrong.
			_error = exception.Message;
			await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
		}
		finally
		{
			_painting = false;
		}
	}

	private void ClearError()
	{
		if (_error is null)
		{
			return;
		}
		_error = null;
		_ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Draws the overlay into an off-screen surface and returns it base64 PNG-encoded, or null
	/// when there is nothing sensible to draw into.
	/// <para>
	/// PNG rather than raw pixels because the frame crosses a JSON bridge on the phone: a
	/// 1600×1200 canvas is 7.7 MB of RGBA and a few tens of kilobytes as PNG, since almost all
	/// of it is transparent. The encode is the cheaper half of that trade by a wide margin.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private string? Rasterise(MapViewport viewport)
	{
		if (viewport.CanvasWidthPx <= 0 || viewport.CanvasHeightPx <= 0)
		{
			return null;
		}

		// The raster target is kept between frames and only rebuilt when the canvas changes
		// size. A phone overlay is a few million pixels; allocating and freeing that buffer on
		// every frame of a pan is pure garbage-collector pressure for a surface whose contents
		// PaintOverlay clears anyway.
		if (_raster is null || _rasterWidth != viewport.CanvasWidthPx || _rasterHeight != viewport.CanvasHeightPx)
		{
			_raster?.Dispose();
			_raster = SKSurface.Create(new SKImageInfo(
				viewport.CanvasWidthPx,
				viewport.CanvasHeightPx,
				SKColorType.Rgba8888,
				SKAlphaType.Premul));

			if (_raster is null)
			{
				return null;
			}

			_rasterWidth = viewport.CanvasWidthPx;
			_rasterHeight = viewport.CanvasHeightPx;
		}

		// Reused surface, so the clear inside PaintOverlay is now load-bearing rather than
		// belt-and-braces: without it every frame would composite over the last one.
		PaintOverlay(_raster.Canvas);
		_raster.Canvas.Flush();

		using SKPixmap pixmap = _raster.PeekPixels();
		using SKDynamicMemoryWStream stream = new();

		if (!pixmap.Encode(stream, new SKPngEncoderOptions(SKPngEncoderFilterFlags.NoFilters, PngZLibLevel)))
		{
			return null;
		}

		using SKData data = stream.DetachAsData();
		return data is null ? null : Convert.ToBase64String(data.AsSpan());
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// zlib level for the frame PNG, and the reason a pan feels quicker than it did.
	/// <para>
	/// The default of 6 — and the 100 this used to pass — spends most of the encode budget
	/// squeezing an image that is about to travel a few hundred microseconds across an in-process
	/// bridge and then be thrown away. Level 1 with filtering off is the fastest lossless setting
	/// Skia offers, and on an overlay that is transparent almost everywhere the extra bytes are a
	/// far cheaper currency than the milliseconds: nothing here is stored, cached or sent over a
	/// network.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private const int PngZLibLevel = 1;

	/// <summary>The raster target, reused across frames. Rebuilt only when the canvas resizes.</summary>
	private SKSurface? _raster;

	private int _rasterWidth;
	private int _rasterHeight;

	private async Task<IJSObjectReference> LoadModuleAsync() =>
		_module ??= await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", ModulePath);

	/// <summary>
	/// Draws each ground circle: a wash inside, a ring on the edge, and a dot on the centre.
	/// <para>
	/// The dot is not decoration. A 1 km circle at street zoom is wider than the canvas, so
	/// without it the only thing on screen is a tint the rider cannot tell from a map style —
	/// and the point they are being asked to position is the centre.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private void DrawCircles(SKCanvas canvas)
	{
		if (Circles is not { Count: > 0 } circles) return;

		foreach (MapCircle circle in circles)
		{
			SKPoint centre = Project(circle.Latitude, circle.Longitude);
			float radius = (float)MapGeometry.MetresToCanvasPixels(Viewport, circle.Latitude, circle.RadiusM);

			SKColor colour = ParseColour(circle.Colour);

			if (radius > 0.5f)
			{
				// Both passes are skipped for a circle drawn entirely off screen — at low zoom a
				// ride's worth of panning can leave the centre thousands of pixels away, and Skia
				// would otherwise rasterise a ring that intersects nothing.
				if (centre.X + radius >= -CanvasMarginPx && centre.X - radius <= Viewport.CanvasWidthPx + CanvasMarginPx
					&& centre.Y + radius >= -CanvasMarginPx && centre.Y - radius <= Viewport.CanvasHeightPx + CanvasMarginPx)
				{
					using SKPaint wash = new()
					{
						Style = SKPaintStyle.Fill,
						IsAntialias = true,
						Color = colour.WithAlpha(CircleWashAlpha),
					};

					using SKPaint ring = new()
					{
						Style = SKPaintStyle.Stroke,
						StrokeWidth = CircleRingWidthPx,
						IsAntialias = true,
						Color = colour,
					};

					canvas.DrawCircle(centre, radius, wash);
					canvas.DrawCircle(centre, radius, ring);
				}
			}

			if (!InsideCanvas(centre)) continue;

			using SKPaint dotEdge = new()
			{
				Style = SKPaintStyle.Stroke,
				StrokeWidth = 2f,
				IsAntialias = true,
				Color = SKColors.White,
			};

			using SKPaint dotFill = new()
			{
				Style = SKPaintStyle.Fill,
				IsAntialias = true,
				Color = colour,
			};

			canvas.DrawCircle(centre, CircleCentreDotRadius, dotFill);
			canvas.DrawCircle(centre, CircleCentreDotRadius, dotEdge);
		}
	}

	/// <summary>How opaque the wash inside a circle is. Enough to read as an area, sheer enough to leave the tiles legible under it.</summary>
	private const byte CircleWashAlpha = 0x33;

	/// <summary>The circle's edge, in canvas pixels.</summary>
	private float CircleRingWidthPx => (2f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>The dot on a circle's centre — small enough to point at a spot, big enough to find.</summary>
	private float CircleCentreDotRadius => (5f) * _scale;

	private void DrawRoutes(SKCanvas canvas)
	{
		if (_decodedRoutes.Count == 0) return;

		// The rider's own answer for this device (§18.6) — colours, thickness and whether the
		// line says which way round it goes. Read once per frame rather than per route: it
		// cannot change mid-paint, and RouteStyleState raises Changed when it does, which is
		// what invalidates this canvas.
		RouteStyle style = RouteStyles.Style;

		float lineWidth = (float)style.LineWidthPx;
		SKColor casingColour = ParseColour(style.OutlineColour);

		// Two paints for every line, with only the fill's colour changed between them. This is
		// the per-frame allocation the static paints below exist to avoid; a route list is short
		// enough that the loop is cheap and long enough that a pair of paints each would not be.
		using SKPaint casing = new()
		{
			Style = SKPaintStyle.Stroke,
			StrokeWidth = lineWidth + CasingWidthPx,
			IsAntialias = true,
			StrokeCap = SKStrokeCap.Round,
			StrokeJoin = SKStrokeJoin.Round,
			Color = casingColour,
		};

		using SKPaint stroke = new()
		{
			Style = SKPaintStyle.Stroke,
			StrokeWidth = lineWidth,
			IsAntialias = true,
			StrokeCap = SKStrokeCap.Round,
			StrokeJoin = SKStrokeJoin.Round,
		};

		foreach (DecodedRoute route in _decodedRoutes)
		{
			if (route.Points.Count < 2) continue;

			// Reversing is a direction to *read* the stored points in, not a second copy of them
			// (§5.4). The stroke comes out identical either way — a line has no ends as far as
			// Skia is concerned — so the only thing this changes is which way the chevrons below
			// point, which is the whole of what the rider asked for. The gap list is reversed at
			// its own call site, against the same flag; see RouteStyleState.IsReversed.
			_projected.Clear();
			if (RouteStyles.IsReversed(route.TrackId))
			{
				for (int index = route.Points.Count - 1; index >= 0; index--)
				{
					(double latitude, double longitude) = route.Points[index];
					_projected.Add(Project(latitude, longitude));
				}
			}
			else
			{
				foreach ((double latitude, double longitude) in route.Points)
				{
					_projected.Add(Project(latitude, longitude));
				}
			}

			_pathBuilder.MoveTo(_projected[0]);
			for (int index = 1; index < _projected.Count; index++)
			{
				_pathBuilder.LineTo(_projected[index]);
			}

			using SKPath path = _pathBuilder.Detach();

			// Most-specific-wins, resolved by RouteStyleState: a colour pinned to this track,
			// then the "same colour for every route" choice, then the palette entry the calling
			// page assigned by position.
			stroke.Color = ParseColour(RouteStyles.ColourFor(route.TrackId, route.PaletteColour));

			// Casing first, under the line and wider than it, so the pair reads as one edged
			// stroke. OSM tiles run from near-white sand to dark green forest and a bare line
			// is legible over about half of them.
			canvas.DrawPath(path, casing);
			canvas.DrawPath(path, stroke);

			if (style.ShowDirectionArrows)
			{
				DrawDirectionArrows(canvas, lineWidth, ParseColour(style.ArrowColour), casingColour);
			}
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Draws chevrons along the projected line in <see cref="_projected"/>, evenly spaced in
	/// <em>screen</em> pixels and pointing the way the route runs.
	/// <para>
	/// Screen-spaced rather than spaced by GPX point, because a GPX's points are as dense as
	/// whatever recorded it: one chevron per point puts a solid ribbon of arrowheads on a
	/// 1 Hz recording and two arrows on a hand-drawn route of the same length.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="canvas">The overlay canvas.</param>
	/// <param name="lineWidth">The line's own width, which the chevrons scale with.</param>
	/// <param name="arrowColour">The rider's chosen chevron colour.</param>
	/// <param name="casingColour">The line's casing colour, reused to edge each chevron so it stays visible on a line of its own colour.</param>
	private void DrawDirectionArrows(SKCanvas canvas, float lineWidth, SKColor arrowColour, SKColor casingColour)
	{
		// Chevrons grow with the line so they still read on a 12 px stroke, but not linearly —
		// a chevron three times the size wants more than three times the gap or the line turns
		// into a chain of arrowheads.
		float scale = Math.Clamp(lineWidth / RouteStyleDefaultWidthPx, 0.8f, 2.2f);
		float spacing = ArrowSpacingPx * scale;

		using SKPaint edge = new()
		{
			Style = SKPaintStyle.Stroke,
			StrokeWidth = (ChevronStrokePx * scale) + 2f,
			IsAntialias = true,
			StrokeCap = SKStrokeCap.Round,
			StrokeJoin = SKStrokeJoin.Round,
			Color = casingColour,
		};

		using SKPaint fill = new()
		{
			Style = SKPaintStyle.Stroke,
			StrokeWidth = ChevronStrokePx * scale,
			IsAntialias = true,
			StrokeCap = SKStrokeCap.Round,
			StrokeJoin = SKStrokeJoin.Round,
			Color = arrowColour,
		};

		// Distance still to run before the next chevron is due. Carried across segments so the
		// spacing is even along the whole line rather than restarting at every GPX point —
		// restarting would put a chevron on every point, which is the failure this method's
		// remarks describe.
		float untilNext = spacing / 2f;

		for (int index = 1; index < _projected.Count; index++)
		{
			SKPoint from = _projected[index - 1];
			SKPoint to = _projected[index];

			float dx = to.X - from.X;
			float dy = to.Y - from.Y;
			float length = MathF.Sqrt((dx * dx) + (dy * dy));

			// Duplicate fixes are common in recorded GPX. A zero-length segment has no
			// direction to point in, and normalising it would divide by zero.
			if (length < 0.001f) continue;

			if (length < untilNext)
			{
				untilNext -= length;
				continue;
			}

			int count = (int)((length - untilNext) / spacing) + 1;

			// Where the next segment picks up. Computed from the arithmetic rather than from
			// wherever the drawing loop below stopped, because that loop only walks the part of
			// the segment that is on screen.
			float carriedOn = untilNext + (count * spacing) - length;

			// A segment can be millions of pixels long at high zoom — the canvas is the small
			// window in the middle of it. Clipping first means the loop runs once per *visible*
			// chevron instead of once per chevron on a line that mostly leaves the country.
			if (ClipToCanvas(from, to, out float enters, out float leaves))
			{
				float bearingDeg = MathF.Atan2(dy, dx) * 180f / MathF.PI;
				float first = enters * length;

				float along = untilNext;
				if (first > along)
				{
					along += MathF.Ceiling((first - along) / spacing) * spacing;
				}

				float last = MathF.Min(leaves * length, length);
				for (; along <= last; along += spacing)
				{
					float fraction = along / length;
					DrawChevron(canvas, new SKPoint(from.X + (dx * fraction), from.Y + (dy * fraction)), bearingDeg, scale, edge, fill);
				}
			}

			untilNext = carriedOn;
		}
	}

	/// <summary>Draws one chevron, rotated onto the segment it sits on.</summary>
	/// <param name="canvas">The overlay canvas.</param>
	/// <param name="at">Where it goes, in canvas pixels.</param>
	/// <param name="bearingDeg">Screen bearing of the segment, degrees clockwise from +X.</param>
	/// <param name="scale">Chevron size relative to the chevron path's own.</param>
	/// <param name="edge">The wider, darker pass.</param>
	/// <param name="fill">The chevron's own colour.</param>
	private void DrawChevron(SKCanvas canvas, SKPoint at, float bearingDeg, float scale, SKPaint edge, SKPaint fill)
	{
		canvas.Save();
		try
		{
			canvas.Translate(at.X, at.Y);

			// (_chevronPath ??= BuildChevronPath()) points along +X, and the bearing is already measured in canvas space
			// (atan2 of the projected delta), so no north-relative conversion happens here —
			// unlike the rider dart, which starts from a compass heading.
			canvas.RotateDegrees(bearingDeg);
			canvas.Scale(scale);

			canvas.DrawPath((_chevronPath ??= BuildChevronPath()), edge);
			canvas.DrawPath((_chevronPath ??= BuildChevronPath()), fill);
		}
		finally
		{
			canvas.Restore();
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Liang–Barsky clip of a segment against the canvas (plus the same margin
	/// <see cref="InsideCanvas"/> uses), as the fraction of the segment that is on screen.
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="from">Segment start, canvas pixels.</param>
	/// <param name="to">Segment end, canvas pixels.</param>
	/// <param name="enters">Fraction along the segment where it comes on screen.</param>
	/// <param name="leaves">Fraction where it goes off again.</param>
	/// <returns><c>false</c> when no part of the segment is visible, in which case the two fractions are meaningless.</returns>
	private bool ClipToCanvas(SKPoint from, SKPoint to, out float enters, out float leaves)
	{
		enters = 0f;
		leaves = 1f;

		float dx = to.X - from.X;
		float dy = to.Y - from.Y;

		// Left, right, top, bottom. Each is "how far the segment may run before it crosses
		// this edge", expressed as the pair Liang–Barsky wants.
		return ClipEdge(-dx, from.X + CanvasMarginPx, ref enters, ref leaves)
			&& ClipEdge(dx, Viewport.CanvasWidthPx + CanvasMarginPx - from.X, ref enters, ref leaves)
			&& ClipEdge(-dy, from.Y + CanvasMarginPx, ref enters, ref leaves)
			&& ClipEdge(dy, Viewport.CanvasHeightPx + CanvasMarginPx - from.Y, ref enters, ref leaves);
	}

	private static bool ClipEdge(float denominator, float numerator, ref float enters, ref float leaves)
	{
		if (denominator == 0f)
		{
			// Parallel to this edge: visible only if it starts on the inside of it.
			return numerator >= 0f;
		}

		float crossing = numerator / denominator;

		if (denominator < 0f)
		{
			if (crossing > leaves) return false;
			if (crossing > enters) enters = crossing;
		}
		else
		{
			if (crossing < enters) return false;
			if (crossing < leaves) leaves = crossing;
		}

		return true;
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// How much wider the casing is than the line it sits under — 1.5 px of edge either side,
	/// which is enough to separate the line from a tile without reading as a second line.
	/// </summary>
	private float CasingWidthPx => (3f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>
	/// The line width the chevron scale is expressed relative to. Deliberately the same number
	/// as <c>RouteStyle.Default.LineWidthPx</c>, so a device on the defaults draws chevrons at
	/// exactly the size <see cref="(_chevronPath ??= BuildChevronPath())"/> is authored at.
	/// </summary>
	private float RouteStyleDefaultWidthPx => (4f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>Gap between direction chevrons along the line, in canvas pixels at scale 1.</summary>
	private float ArrowSpacingPx => (72f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>Stroke width of a chevron at scale 1.</summary>
	private float ChevronStrokePx => (2.2f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>How far outside the canvas something may sit and still be worth drawing.</summary>
	private float CanvasMarginPx => (32f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>
	/// The direction chevron: an open "greater-than" pointing along +X, centred on the point it
	/// is drawn at. Open rather than a filled triangle because it is stroked in the same round
	/// cap the line uses, so at any size it reads as part of the line rather than as a marker
	/// dropped on it — and the line's own colour shows through the middle.
	/// <para>
	/// Built once, for the same reason <see cref="(_arrowPath ??= BuildArrowPath())"/> is: a long route at low zoom
	/// carries dozens of these per frame.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private SKPath? _chevronPath;

	/// <summary>
	/// The <see cref="_scale"/> the cached paths below were built at.
	/// <para>
	/// They are unit geometry authored in canvas pixels, so a device-pixel-ratio change
	/// invalidates every one of them. NaN until the first paint, so the first frame always
	/// builds.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private float _pathScale = float.NaN;

	/// <summary>Discards the cached geometry when the scale it was authored at no longer applies.</summary>
	private void EnsurePathsMatchScale()
	{
		if (_pathScale.Equals(_scale))
		{
			return;
		}

		_chevronPath?.Dispose();
		_riderArrowPath?.Dispose();
		_riderDotPath?.Dispose();
		_arrowPath?.Dispose();
		_chevronPath = _riderArrowPath = _riderDotPath = _arrowPath = null;
		_pathScale = _scale;
	}

	private SKPath BuildChevronPath()
	{
		using SKPathBuilder builder = new();
		builder.MoveTo(-ChevronHalfLength, -ChevronHalfSpan);
		builder.LineTo(ChevronHalfLength, 0f);
		builder.LineTo(-ChevronHalfLength, ChevronHalfSpan);
		return builder.Detach();
	}

	/// <summary>Chevron tip to the line through its two tails, along the direction of travel.</summary>
	private float ChevronHalfLength => (3.5f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>Half the span across the chevron's two tails.</summary>
	private float ChevronHalfSpan => (4.5f) * _scale;

	private void DrawMarkers(SKCanvas canvas)
	{
		if (Markers is null) return;

		SKSamplingOptions sampling = new(SKFilterMode.Linear, SKMipmapMode.Linear);

		// Riders carry a colour each (§16.3), so the paints that draw them cannot be the shared
		// static ones below — those are immutable configuration precisely so nothing mutates them
		// per marker. One pair for the whole frame, recoloured per rider: a twenty-rider map at
		// 1 Hz would otherwise build forty native paints a second for no gain.
		using SKPaint riderFill = new() { Style = SKPaintStyle.Fill, IsAntialias = true };
		using SKPaint riderStroke = new() { Style = SKPaintStyle.Stroke, IsAntialias = true, StrokeJoin = SKStrokeJoin.Round };

		foreach (MapMarker marker in Markers.Values)
		{
			SKPoint screen = Project(marker.Latitude, marker.Longitude);
			if (!InsideCanvas(screen)) continue;

			if (marker.Kind == MarkerKind.Rider)
			{
				DrawRider(canvas, screen, marker, riderFill, riderStroke);

				continue;
			}

			if (marker.Kind == MarkerKind.Self)
			{
				DrawSelf(canvas, screen, marker, riderFill, riderStroke);

				continue;
			}

			// Null means "no direction", never zero — zero is due north, a real bearing
			// (§16.2). A marker that carries no bearing gets no arrow rather than one
			// confidently pointing north.
			bool hasHeading = marker.DirectionDeg is not null;

			// An authored marker may also carry a bearing ("face this way", §16.2). It
			// gets the same arrow at reduced scale, pushed clear of the icon disc, so it
			// decorates the icon rather than replacing it.
			if (marker.DirectionDeg is { } markerBearing)
			{
				DrawHeadingArrow(canvas, screen, markerBearing, MarkerArrowScale, MarkerArrowOffset);
			}

			SKImage? glyph = GlyphImage(marker.IconKey);

			if (glyph is null)
			{
				// Icon not rasterised yet (or the host cannot). A plain pin holds the
				// place — the marker is still on the map and still labelled.
				canvas.DrawCircle(screen, 6f, PinFill);
				canvas.DrawCircle(screen, 6f, PinRing);
			}
			else
			{
				// Straight onto the map, no disc behind it. Each icon carries its own 1px black
				// and 1px white outline, which is the job the plate used to do — and it does it
				// following the artwork's edge rather than boxing it into a circle, so the
				// silhouette is what a rider recognises at a glance instead of "a white dot".
				SKRect target = new(
					screen.X - IconSize / 2, screen.Y - IconSize / 2,
					screen.X + IconSize / 2, screen.Y + IconSize / 2);
				canvas.DrawImage(glyph, target, sampling);
			}

			if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(marker.Title))
			{
				// Clear whatever was drawn above, so a south-facing marker's title is never
				// printed over its own bearing arrow.
				float clearance = hasHeading
					? MarkerArrowOffset + (ArrowTipLength * MarkerArrowScale) + LabelGap
					: (IconSize / 2) + LabelGap + 2f;

				float ty = screen.Y + clearance;
				canvas.DrawText(marker.Title, screen.X, ty, SKTextAlign.Center, LabelFont, LabelHalo);
				canvas.DrawText(marker.Title, screen.X, ty, SKTextAlign.Center, LabelFont, LabelText);
			}
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Draws one rider: their name on a pill in their own colour, with the pill's rounded end
	/// centred on the fix, and on top of that end either an arrow pointing the way they are going
	/// or a dot saying they have stopped (§5.3, §16.3).
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>The label runs to the right of the position, not under it.</strong> Twenty riders
	/// on a phone screen at regroup are twenty overlapping pins; a name centred beneath each one
	/// puts every label in every other label's way, while a pill anchored at the point keeps the
	/// thing being named and the name in one shape that the eye can follow apart from its
	/// neighbours.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>The rider picks the background and nothing else.</strong> Text and border are
	/// <see cref="MarkerColours.ForegroundFor"/>'s answer — black or white, whichever reads —
	/// because a rider free to choose both could pick a pairing that disappears, and the person
	/// who pays for that is whoever is looking for them at a junction.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="canvas">The overlay canvas.</param>
	/// <param name="at">Where the rider is, in canvas pixels.</param>
	/// <param name="marker">The rider's marker, carrying their colour, speed and heading.</param>
	/// <param name="fill">The frame's shared fill paint. Recoloured here.</param>
	/// <param name="stroke">The frame's shared stroke paint. Recoloured and re-widthed here.</param>
	private void DrawRider(SKCanvas canvas, SKPoint at, MapMarker marker, SKPaint fill, SKPaint stroke)
	{
		SKColor back = ParseColour(MarkerColours.Or(marker.Colour));
		SKColor fore = ParseColour(MarkerColours.ForegroundFor(marker.Colour));

		string text = RiderMarker.Label(marker.Title);

		float textWidth = LabelFont.MeasureText(text) + LabelPadPx;

		// The pill: a rectangle to the right, closed on the left by a semicircle centred on the
		// fix. Skia measures the arc clockwise from +X, so 90° → 270° is the left half.
		_pathBuilder.MoveTo(at.X, at.Y - LabelHalfHeight);
		_pathBuilder.LineTo(at.X + LabelHalfHeight + textWidth, at.Y - LabelHalfHeight);
		_pathBuilder.LineTo(at.X + LabelHalfHeight + textWidth, at.Y + LabelHalfHeight);
		_pathBuilder.LineTo(at.X, at.Y + LabelHalfHeight);
		_pathBuilder.ArcTo(
			new SKRect(at.X - LabelHalfHeight, at.Y - LabelHalfHeight, at.X + LabelHalfHeight, at.Y + LabelHalfHeight),
			90,
			180,
			false);
		_pathBuilder.Close();

		using SKPath pill = _pathBuilder.Detach();

		fill.Color = back;
		canvas.DrawPath(pill, fill);

		stroke.Color = fore;
		stroke.StrokeWidth = LabelBorderPx;
		canvas.DrawPath(pill, stroke);

		fill.Color = fore;
		canvas.DrawText(text, at.X + LabelHalfHeight + LabelInsetPx, at.Y + LabelBaselineOffset, SKTextAlign.Left, LabelFont, fill);

		// Arrow or dot, drawn last so it sits on top of the pill's rounded end rather than being
		// half-covered by it.
		bool moving = RiderMarker.IsMoving(marker.SpeedMps, marker.DirectionDeg);

		canvas.Save();
		try
		{
			canvas.Translate(at.X, at.Y);

			if (moving)
			{
				// Canvas Y grows downward, so screen-up is -Y — which is where (_riderArrowPath ??= BuildRiderArrowPath())
				// points — and a clockwise rotation by the screen bearing carries the tip round
				// to the compass point. Same conversion the authored-marker arrow uses, so a
				// heading-up map turns both together.
				canvas.RotateDegrees((float)MapGeometry.ScreenBearingDeg(marker.DirectionDeg!.Value, Viewport.HeadingDeg));
			}

			SKPath shape = moving ? (_riderArrowPath ??= BuildRiderArrowPath()) : (_riderDotPath ??= BuildRiderDotPath());

			// Three passes, widest first. The fore-coloured outline separates the shape from the
			// rider's own label colour, the fill says moving or stopped, and the hairline in the
			// opposite ink keeps the two apart on a label whose colour happens to be close to
			// either of them.
			stroke.Color = fore;
			stroke.StrokeWidth = GpsEdgePx;
			canvas.DrawPath(shape, stroke);

			fill.Color = moving ? RiderMovingColour : RiderStoppedColour;
			canvas.DrawPath(shape, fill);

			stroke.Color = fore == SKColors.Black ? SKColors.White : SKColors.Black;
			stroke.StrokeWidth = GpsHairlinePx;
			canvas.DrawPath(shape, stroke);
		}
		finally
		{
			canvas.Restore();
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Draws <em>you</em>: an arrowhead along the way this device is facing when the fix says which
	/// way, and a green dot when it does not — with no label behind either, and half as big again
	/// as a rider pin (§4.3).
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>No name, and that is the whole distinction.</strong> Every other position on this
	/// map is a pill with somebody's name on it; a rider does not need to be told their own, and a
	/// bare shape is instantly legible as "me" in a knot of pins at a regroup. It is also the one
	/// marker on the map that is not a claim about what anyone else can see — it comes off this
	/// phone's receiver, not off a published fix (§5.3).
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Larger than a rider pin on purpose. It sits under the same finger the rider is panning with,
	/// often through a visor, and it is the thing they look for first when they open the map.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="canvas">The overlay canvas.</param>
	/// <param name="at">Where this device is, in canvas pixels.</param>
	/// <param name="marker">The self marker. Only its heading is read — see the note below on why not its speed.</param>
	/// <param name="fill">The frame's shared fill paint. Recoloured here.</param>
	/// <param name="stroke">The frame's shared stroke paint. Recoloured and re-widthed here.</param>
	private void DrawSelf(SKCanvas canvas, SKPoint at, MapMarker marker, SKPaint fill, SKPaint stroke)
	{
		// The fix carrying a direction is the whole test, and the speed is not consulted —
		// deliberately unlike a rider pin, which demands both (RiderMarker.IsMoving).
		//
		// The difference is what the two marks are drawn from. A rider pin comes off a fix that has
		// been round the server and arrives as whole metres per second, so the speed is the only
		// evidence left that a bearing means anything. This one is *this* phone's own live fix, and
		// both platforms usually decline to report a bearing they cannot stand behind — iOS returns
		// Course as -1 when stationary, Android answers false to HasBearing — with both providers
		// mapping that to null rather than to zero (§8). So the receiver's own "I do not know which
		// way" is the better signal here, and it is the one a rider asked to see.
		//
		// If a fused provider is ever seen handing back a jittery bearing at a standstill, the fix
		// belongs in the provider that produced it, not in a speed guess bolted on here.
		bool pointing = marker.DirectionDeg is not null;

		canvas.Save();
		try
		{
			canvas.Translate(at.X, at.Y);
			canvas.Scale(SelfScale);

			if (pointing)
			{
				canvas.RotateDegrees((float)MapGeometry.ScreenBearingDeg(marker.DirectionDeg!.Value, Viewport.HeadingDeg));
			}

			SKPath shape = pointing ? (_riderArrowPath ??= BuildRiderArrowPath()) : (_riderDotPath ??= BuildRiderDotPath());

			// Three passes, widest first — the same sandwich the heading arrow uses, and for the
			// same reason: OSM runs from near-white sand to dark forest, and a single-outlined
			// shape disappears into one end of that or the other. White outermost because this one
			// sits on tiles rather than on a coloured pill.
			//
			// The widths are divided by the scale so the canvas transform above does not thicken
			// them with the shape — a stroke is a constant on screen, not a proportion of the mark.
			stroke.Color = SKColors.White;
			stroke.StrokeWidth = (GpsEdgePx + GpsHairlinePx) / SelfScale;
			canvas.DrawPath(shape, stroke);

			fill.Color = pointing ? SelfPointingColour : SelfStoppedColour;
			canvas.DrawPath(shape, fill);

			stroke.Color = SKColors.Black;
			stroke.StrokeWidth = GpsHairlinePx / SelfScale;
			canvas.DrawPath(shape, stroke);
		}
		finally
		{
			canvas.Restore();
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// How much larger the own-position mark is than a rider's. See <see cref="DrawSelf"/> — it is
	/// the one somebody is looking for through a visor.
	/// </summary>
	private const float SelfScale = 1.5f;

	/// <summary>
	/// The own-position arrowhead: the same blue the moving-rider dart and the authored-marker
	/// arrow use, so one colour goes on meaning "the app drew this" across the overlay.
	/// </summary>
	private static readonly SKColor SelfPointingColour = new(0x25, 0x63, 0xeb);

	/// <summary>
	/// The own-position dot, when the receiver has not said which way this phone is facing.
	/// <para>
	/// Green, and pointedly <em>not</em> the red a stopped rider gets (<see cref="RiderStoppedColour"/>).
	/// That red is a call for attention — somebody else on the ride has stopped and the group may
	/// need to know. Your own phone having no bearing yet is neither a problem nor anybody's
	/// business: it is a working GPS that has not been moved. Red on the mark a rider looks at most
	/// would be crying wolf every time they sat at a light.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private static readonly SKColor SelfStoppedColour = new(0x16, 0xa3, 0x4a);

	// Paints and fonts are immutable configuration built from compile-time constants, and
	// this method runs on every viewport change with live positions arriving about once a
	// second. Building eleven native SkPaint objects per frame — each one a native alloc plus
	// a registration in SkiaSharp's global handle dictionary — bought nothing. Held for the
	// process lifetime and deliberately never disposed: there is no static-teardown hook to
	// hang a dispose off, and the native memory goes back at exit. Nothing may mutate them
	// per-marker; per-marker variation is done with canvas transforms.
	private static readonly SKPaint PinFill = new() { Style = SKPaintStyle.Fill, IsAntialias = true, Color = new SKColor(0x25, 0x63, 0xeb) };
	private static readonly SKPaint LabelText = new() { IsAntialias = true, Color = SKColors.Black };

	// Instance, not static, because their sizes now depend on the device pixel ratio of the
	// frame being drawn (see _scale). Two overlays on two hosts would otherwise fight over one
	// stroke width, and the loser would draw at the other's scale.
	private readonly SKPaint PinRing = new() { Style = SKPaintStyle.Stroke, IsAntialias = true, Color = SKColors.White };
	private readonly SKFont LabelFont = new();
	private readonly SKPaint LabelHalo = new() { IsAntialias = true, Color = SKColors.White, Style = SKPaintStyle.Stroke };

	/// <summary>The label's type size in CSS pixels, before <see cref="_scale"/> is applied.</summary>
	private const float LabelFontCssPx = 12f;

	// -- The rider pill (§16.3) ---------------------------------------------------------------
	//
	// Everything below is sized off LabelFont so the pill is exactly tall enough for the name in
	// it. Declared after the font on purpose: static initialisers run in source order, and a
	// height derived from a font that has not been built yet is zero.

	/// <summary>
	/// Half the pill's height, which is also the radius of its rounded end and therefore the
	/// radius the arrow and the dot are drawn within. One number, so the shape stays a pill and
	/// the marker on it stays centred on the fix however the font size changes.
	/// </summary>
	private float LabelHalfHeight => (LabelFont.Size / 2f) + (1f * _scale);

	/// <summary>
	/// Baseline offset from the fix, so the name sits optically centred in the pill rather than
	/// hanging off its top edge. Derived from the font's own ascent and descent — a hand-tuned
	/// constant here is a constant that has to be re-tuned when the size changes.
	/// </summary>
	private float LabelBaselineOffset => -(LabelFont.Metrics.Ascent + LabelFont.Metrics.Descent) / 2f;

	/// <summary>Breathing room after the text, so the pill does not end flush against the last letter.</summary>
	private float LabelPadPx => (4f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>Gap between the rounded end and the first letter.</summary>
	private float LabelInsetPx => (2f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>The pill's border, drawn in the same ink as its text.</summary>
	private float LabelBorderPx => (1f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>Outline around the arrow or dot, in the label's ink.</summary>
	private float GpsEdgePx => (2f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>The thin opposite-ink line inside that outline. See <c>DrawRider</c>.</summary>
	private float GpsHairlinePx => (1f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>
	/// Travelling: the same blue the authored-marker arrow uses, so one shape means one thing
	/// across the whole overlay.
	/// </summary>
	private static readonly SKColor RiderMovingColour = new(0x25, 0x63, 0xeb);

	/// <summary>
	/// Stopped, or moving too slowly to claim a direction (<see cref="RiderMarker.MovingAtLeastMps"/>).
	/// Red because a stopped rider on a group ride is the one somebody needs to notice.
	/// </summary>
	private static readonly SKColor RiderStoppedColour = new(0xdc, 0x26, 0x26);

	/// <summary>
	/// The rider's arrow: pointing up (screen-up is -Y), centred on the fix, and sized to sit
	/// within the pill's rounded end. Swallow-tailed for the reason <see cref="(_arrowPath ??= BuildArrowPath())"/> gives
	/// — a plain triangle stops reading as a direction once it is rotated away from vertical.
	/// <para>
	/// Its own path rather than a scaled <see cref="(_arrowPath ??= BuildArrowPath())"/>: this one has to fit a circle the
	/// font decides the size of, and a scale factor between the two is a number nobody could
	/// re-derive later.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private SKPath? _riderArrowPath;

	private SKPath BuildRiderArrowPath()
	{
		// Tip 1.5 radii ahead; rear corners 1.2 radii back at ±135° from the heading, which puts
		// them at 0.8485 (= 1.2 × cos 45°) either way; the tail notch half a radius behind.
		const float tip = 1.5f;
		const float corner = 0.8485f;
		const float notch = 0.5f;

		using SKPathBuilder builder = new();
		builder.MoveTo(0f, -tip * LabelHalfHeight);
		builder.LineTo(corner * LabelHalfHeight, corner * LabelHalfHeight);
		builder.LineTo(0f, notch * LabelHalfHeight);
		builder.LineTo(-corner * LabelHalfHeight, corner * LabelHalfHeight);
		builder.Close();

		return builder.Detach();
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// The stopped dot: a disc inside the pill's rounded end, small enough that the label's border
	/// still reads as a ring around it.
	/// </summary>
	private SKPath? _riderDotPath;

	private SKPath BuildRiderDotPath()
	{
		using SKPathBuilder builder = new();
		builder.AddCircle(0f, 0f, LabelHalfHeight - 2f, SKPathDirection.Clockwise);

		return builder.Detach();
	}

	// The heading arrow: blue, edged in black, haloed in white. Three passes over one path —
	// widest stroke first — so the black reads as the arrow's own outline and the white
	// separates it from whatever tile is underneath. OSM runs from near-white sand to dark
	// forest, and a single-outlined arrow disappears into one end or the other.
	private readonly SKPaint ArrowHalo = new() { Style = SKPaintStyle.Stroke, IsAntialias = true, StrokeJoin = SKStrokeJoin.Round, Color = SKColors.White };
	private readonly SKPaint ArrowEdge = new() { Style = SKPaintStyle.Stroke, IsAntialias = true, StrokeJoin = SKStrokeJoin.Round, Color = SKColors.Black };
	private static readonly SKPaint ArrowFill = new() { Style = SKPaintStyle.Fill, IsAntialias = true, Color = new SKColor(0x25, 0x63, 0xeb) };

	/// <summary>How much smaller the arrow is when it decorates an authored marker's icon.</summary>
	private const float MarkerArrowScale = 0.55f;

	/// <summary>How far that arrow is pushed along the bearing to clear the icon.</summary>
	private float MarkerArrowOffset => (IconSize / 2) + (6f * _scale);

	/// <summary>Gap between whatever was drawn and the top of the label beneath it.</summary>
	private float LabelGap => (11f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>
	/// Draws the bearing dart an authored marker may carry — "face this way" (§16.2). Riders get
	/// <c>(_riderArrowPath ??= BuildRiderArrowPath())</c> instead, which is sized to their label rather than to an icon disc.
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="canvas">The overlay canvas.</param>
	/// <param name="centre">Where the marker is, in canvas pixels.</param>
	/// <param name="bearingDeg">Degrees clockwise from true north.</param>
	/// <param name="scale">Size relative to <see cref="(_arrowPath ??= BuildArrowPath())"/>'s own.</param>
	/// <param name="forwardOffset">Push along the bearing, to clear an icon disc. Unscaled pixels.</param>
	private void DrawHeadingArrow(
		SKCanvas canvas,
		SKPoint centre,
		double bearingDeg,
		float scale,
		float forwardOffset)
	{
		canvas.Save();
		try
		{
			canvas.Translate(centre.X, centre.Y);

			// Canvas Y grows downward, so screen-up is -Y and a clockwise rotation by the
			// screen bearing carries the tip round to the compass point.
			canvas.RotateDegrees((float)MapGeometry.ScreenBearingDeg(bearingDeg, Viewport.HeadingDeg));

			if (forwardOffset != 0f)
			{
				canvas.Translate(0f, -forwardOffset);
			}

			if (scale != 1f)
			{
				canvas.Scale(scale);
			}

			// Widest stroke first: white halo, then black outline, then the blue interior
			// paints over the inner half of both. Strokes are centred on the path, so the
			// visible result is blue edged in black and separated from the map by white.
			canvas.DrawPath((_arrowPath ??= BuildArrowPath()), ArrowHalo);
			canvas.DrawPath((_arrowPath ??= BuildArrowPath()), ArrowEdge);
			canvas.DrawPath((_arrowPath ??= BuildArrowPath()), ArrowFill);
		}
		finally
		{
			canvas.Restore();
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// The dart, pointing up (screen-up is -Y), centred on the marker's position. Four points:
	/// tip, the two rear corners, and a notch between them — the swallowtail is what makes
	/// the pointing end unambiguous at a glance, which a plain triangle is not once it is
	/// rotated away from vertical.
	/// <para>
	/// Built once. It is immutable, and rebuilding a path per marker per frame is the sort of
	/// allocation a twenty-rider map at 1 Hz notices.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private SKPath? _arrowPath;

	private SKPath BuildArrowPath()
	{
		using SKPathBuilder builder = new();
		builder.MoveTo(0f, -ArrowTipLength);
		builder.LineTo(ArrowHalfWidth, ArrowTailLength);
		builder.LineTo(0f, ArrowNotchDepth);
		builder.LineTo(-ArrowHalfWidth, ArrowTailLength);
		builder.Close();
		return builder.Detach();
	}

	/// <summary>Centre to tip, along the heading.</summary>
	private float ArrowTipLength => (14f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>Centre to the rear corners, against the heading.</summary>
	private float ArrowTailLength => (8f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>How far the tail notch cuts back toward the tip.</summary>
	private float ArrowNotchDepth => (1.5f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>Half the span across the rear corners.</summary>
	private float ArrowHalfWidth => (9f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>
	/// The icon bitmaps, wrapped as Skia images. <see cref="MarkerIconCache"/> deliberately
	/// holds only raw RGBA — no image decoder runs outside the photo-ingest path — so the
	/// conversion to an <see cref="SKImage"/> happens here, in the one component §4.5 puts in
	/// the drawing business. This is a pixel copy, not a decode. Built once per icon and kept:
	/// 29 curated icons at 48² is well under a megabyte, and rebuilding per frame would be the
	/// expensive mistake.
	/// </summary>
	private static readonly Dictionary<string, SKImage> GlyphImages = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

	private static SKImage? GlyphImage(string iconKey)
	{
		if (GlyphImages.TryGetValue(iconKey, out SKImage? cached))
		{
			return cached;
		}

		byte[]? rgba = MarkerIconCache.TryGetPixels(iconKey);
		if (rgba is null)
		{
			return null;
		}

		// Unpremultiplied because that is what a 2D canvas' getImageData hands back.
		SKImageInfo info = new(
			MarkerIconCache.RasterSize,
			MarkerIconCache.RasterSize,
			SKColorType.Rgba8888,
			SKAlphaType.Unpremul);

		SKImage? image = SKImage.FromPixelCopy(info, rgba);
		if (image is not null)
		{
			GlyphImages[iconKey] = image;
		}

		return image;
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Drawn icon edge in canvas units. Readable at arm's length on a bar-mounted phone.
	/// <para>
	/// Nothing is drawn behind it, so this is the mark's whole footprint on the map rather than
	/// the size of something sitting on a backing shape.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private float IconSize => (28f) * _scale;

	/// <summary>
	/// Web Mercator projection from geographic degrees to overlay pixel coordinates.
	/// <para>
	/// The arithmetic lives in <see cref="MapGeometry.ProjectToCanvas"/> rather than here,
	/// because the live ride page hit-tests taps against the pins this method draws — and two
	/// copies of a projection agree right up until somebody rotates the map.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private SKPoint Project(double lat, double lon)
	{
		CanvasPoint point = MapGeometry.ProjectToCanvas(Viewport, lat, lon);
		return new SKPoint((float)point.X, (float)point.Y);
	}

	private bool InsideCanvas(SKPoint p) =>
		p.X >= -CanvasMarginPx && p.Y >= -CanvasMarginPx
		&& p.X <= Viewport.CanvasWidthPx + CanvasMarginPx && p.Y <= Viewport.CanvasHeightPx + CanvasMarginPx;

	private static SKColor ParseColour(string hex)
	{
		// Accept #RRGGBB. Anything else falls back to the design's primary blue.
		if (hex.StartsWith('#') && hex.Length == 7
			&& byte.TryParse(hex.AsSpan(1, 2), System.Globalization.NumberStyles.HexNumber, null, out byte r)
			&& byte.TryParse(hex.AsSpan(3, 2), System.Globalization.NumberStyles.HexNumber, null, out byte g)
			&& byte.TryParse(hex.AsSpan(5, 2), System.Globalization.NumberStyles.HexNumber, null, out byte b))
		{
			return new SKColor(r, g, b);
		}
		return new SKColor(0x25, 0x63, 0xeb);
	}

	public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
	{
		RouteStyles.Changed -= OnRouteStyleChanged;
		_pathBuilder.Dispose();

		// Native Skia handles, and instance-owned since v0.24 because their sizes depend on the
		// device pixel ratio. As process-wide statics they never needed releasing; one per
		// <RideMap> does, or every navigation into a ride page leaks a handful.
		_chevronPath?.Dispose();
		_riderArrowPath?.Dispose();
		_riderDotPath?.Dispose();
		_arrowPath?.Dispose();

		// Held across frames since the pan-performance change, so it is this component's to
		// release — a few million pixels per <RideMap> otherwise leaks on every navigation.
		_raster?.Dispose();

		PinRing.Dispose();
		LabelFont.Dispose();
		LabelHalo.Dispose();
		ArrowHalo.Dispose();
		ArrowEdge.Dispose();

		// One module reference per overlay, and an overlay is created per <RideMap>. Leaving it
		// undisposed leaks a tracked JS object on every navigation into a ride page — the same
		// trap the base-map interop documents.
		if (_module is not null)
		{
			// Unhook the move subscription first. It is held by the JS module against the
			// canvas, so disposing the module reference without it leaves a listener attached
			// to a live map for a canvas that has left the DOM — twenty visits to a ride page
			// is twenty of them, all transforming an element nobody can see.
			try { await _module.InvokeVoidAsync("detach", _surface); }
			catch (JSDisconnectedException) { /* WebView or circuit already gone */ }
			catch (ObjectDisposedException) { /* already released */ }
			catch (JSException) { /* module never finished loading */ }

			try { await _module.DisposeAsync(); }
			catch (JSDisconnectedException) { /* WebView or circuit already gone */ }
			catch (ObjectDisposedException) { /* already released */ }
			_module = null;
		}
	}
}

<style>
	/*
	  The canvas sits over the base map. `pointer-events: none` lets pan and zoom
	  travel through to whichever base map is underneath — the overlay never eats
	  gestures, because on every host the base map already handles them and the
	  overlay is a visual layer, not an input surface.
	*/
	.dlr-map-overlay {
		position: absolute;
		inset: 0;
		pointer-events: none;
		width: 100%;
		height: 100%;

		/*
		  The canvas backing store is sized in device pixels by overlay.js and laid out at
		  the container's CSS size by the two rules above, which is what keeps a pin drawn
		  at Viewport.CanvasWidthPx landing on the tile pixel under it.
		*/

		/*
		  Load-bearing, not decorative. overlay.js follows the map by transforming this
		  element, and it measures the translation from the canvas centre — the pixel the
		  painted view's centre coordinate landed on. This is the CSS default, stated
		  because changing it silently offsets every tracked frame by half the map.
		*/
		transform-origin: 50% 50%;

		/*
		  The transform is recomputed on the map's own move event, so the browser must not
		  interpolate between two positions that are already one frame apart — an eased
		  overlay chasing an un-eased map is the drag this tracking exists to remove.
		*/
		transition: none;
	}
</style>
